Subject: [PATCH] OvmfPkg: a TD's waiting APs sleep where somebody will wake them

A TD's processors other than the first wait at a mailbox: in the firmware's
own loop until its DXE phase moves them, and then in a relocated loop until
an OS sets a command for them, and again whenever the OS hands one back.
They wait spinning, the relocated loop without a PAUSE, since nothing could
wake them from a halt: their APICs are off and a TD's vCPU takes no INIT. A
vCPU that spins costs its host a processor for as long as it waits.

Under KVM something can wake them: the hypercall that kicks a halted vCPU,
named by its APIC ID, works whatever state that APIC is in. So an AP now
halts, with the flag that says its interrupts are off, wherever whoever
sets what it waits for kicks it after:

- At the firmware's own mailbox, from the start. Its BSP kicks after each
  command and each release of the APs. The one thing an AP waits for there
  that another AP brings about is the count of those that have arrived: the
  AP that makes it whole kicks the others, since the BSP may have counted
  itself, and kicked, before that AP came.
- At the relocated mailbox while the firmware still runs. No command comes
  then. As boot services end the BSP clears a word of the mailbox and then
  kicks, in that order, so an AP that read the word as set and has not
  halted yet is kicked too.
- At the relocated mailbox under an OS, only once the OS has written
  "KVMKICKS" at the start of the mailbox's area for the OS, which says that
  it kicks an AP after it sets a command for it. Under an OS that never
  writes it the APs spin as before, now with a PAUSE. An OS that writes it
  and can hand the APs to another OS clears it first.

An AP asks its host's KVM leaf whether a kick is offered and spins where it
is not, and the firmware takes nothing else of KVM's. The BSP kicks the APIC
IDs below the TD's MAX_VCPUS, so an AP with another ID spins wherever the
BSP is the one to wake it. A kick that comes before the halt ends that halt
at once, so none is missed between an AP's look at the mailbox and its
halt, and a kick that comes for nothing costs a second look. What the halt
and the kick return is not read.

The one wait this leaves spinning is an AP's for the BSP's page tables, in
the reset vector, which is over in microseconds: it gets a PAUSE.

edk2: edk2-stable202608
---
--- a/OvmfPkg/Include/IndustryStandard/IntelTdx.h
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Include/IndustryStandard/IntelTdx.h
@@ -45,7 +45,13 @@
   UINT64    WakeUpArgs2;
   UINT64    WakeUpArgs3;
   UINT64    WakeUpArgs4;
-  UINT8     Pad1[0xe0];
+  //
+  // Not zero in the relocated mailbox for as long as the firmware runs: no
+  // OS sets a command there yet, and the BSP kicks the APs as it hands the
+  // machine over (see TdxCommondefs.inc).
+  //
+  UINT64    FirmwareRuns;
+  UINT8     Pad1[0xd8];
   UINT64    NumCpusArriving;
   UINT8     Pad2[0xf8];
   UINT64    NumCpusExiting;
--- a/OvmfPkg/Include/Library/TdxMailboxLib.h
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Include/Library/TdxMailboxLib.h
@@ -71,3 +71,14 @@
 MpSerializeEnd (
   VOID
   );
+
+/**
+  This function will be called by BSP to wake the APs that sleep at a mailbox,
+  after it has changed what they wait for. Under a host that does not wake a
+  halted vCPU for a kick no AP sleeps, and this does nothing.
+**/
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+MpKickAps (
+  VOID
+  );
--- a/OvmfPkg/Include/TdxCommondefs.inc
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Include/TdxCommondefs.inc
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 AcceptPageArgsPhysicalEnd                 equ       808h
 AcceptPageArgsTopStackAddress             equ       810h
 AcceptPageArgsApStackSize                 equ       818h
+FirmwareRunsOffset                        equ       820h
 CpuArrivalOffset                          equ       900h
 CpusExitingOffset                         equ       0a00h
 TalliesOffset                             equ       0a08h
@@ -48,3 +49,132 @@
 
 %define TDCALL_TDINFO                          0x1
 %define TDCALL_TDACCEPTPAGE                    0x6
+
+;
+; A waiting AP sleeps where whoever sets what it waits for kicks it after.
+; A kick is KVM's hypercall that makes a halted vCPU runnable: it names the
+; vCPU by its APIC ID and works whatever state that APIC is in.
+;
+; The BSP kicks after each change to the mailbox the firmware starts with,
+; so an AP sleeps at that one from the start. One thing an AP waits for
+; there another AP can bring about: the count of those that have arrived,
+; which the last to arrive makes whole. That AP kicks the others, as the BSP
+; would. At the relocated mailbox only
+; an OS sets commands. Until the firmware hands the machine to one, no
+; command comes, and an AP sleeps there: the BSP clears FirmwareRuns and
+; then kicks the APs as boot services end. From then on an AP sleeps only
+; once the OS has written MP_WAKEUP_OS_KICKS at the start of the mailbox's
+; area for the OS, which says that it kicks an AP after it sets a command.
+; An OS that never writes it finds the APs spinning, as it always did. An
+; OS that writes it and can hand the APs to another OS clears it first.
+;
+; The BSP kicks the APIC IDs below the TD's MAX_VCPUS: an AP with another
+; ID spins wherever the BSP is the one to wake it. Under a host that offers
+; no kick an AP spins everywhere. Every spin has a PAUSE.
+;
+; A kick is a reason to look at the mailbox again and nothing else: what the
+; halt and the kick return is not read.
+;
+%define TDVMCALL_CPUID                         10
+%define TDVMCALL_HLT                           12
+%define KVM_CPUID_SIGNATURE                    0x40000000
+%define KVM_CPUID_FEATURES                     0x40000001
+%define KVM_FEATURE_PV_UNHALT                  7
+%define KVM_HC_KICK_CPU                        5
+%define MP_WAKEUP_OS_KICKS                     0x534b43494b4d564b  ; "KVMKICKS"
+
+;
+; ap_asks_cpuid <leaf>: the host's answer for a CPUID leaf, EAX to EDX in
+; R12D to R15D. ZF is clear if the call failed. Uses RAX, RCX and R10 to
+; R15, and shows the host no other register.
+;
+%macro ap_asks_cpuid 1
+    xor     eax, eax
+    mov     ecx, 0xfc00
+    xor     r10d, r10d
+    mov     r11d, TDVMCALL_CPUID
+    mov     r12d, %1
+    xor     r13d, r13d
+    xor     r14d, r14d
+    xor     r15d, r15d
+    tdcall
+    or      rax, r10
+%endmacro
+
+;
+; ap_may_sleep: R13 is 1 if this AP's host wakes a halted vCPU for a kick,
+; and 0 if not. Uses RAX, RCX and R10 to R15.
+;
+%macro ap_may_sleep 0
+    ap_asks_cpuid KVM_CPUID_SIGNATURE
+    jnz     %%spins
+    cmp     r13d, 0x4b4d564b                ; "KVMK"
+    jne     %%spins
+    cmp     r14d, 0x564b4d56                ; "VMKV"
+    jne     %%spins
+    cmp     r15d, 0x0000004d                ; "M"
+    jne     %%spins
+    ap_asks_cpuid KVM_CPUID_FEATURES
+    jnz     %%spins
+    bt      r12d, KVM_FEATURE_PV_UNHALT
+    jnc     %%spins
+    mov     r13d, 1
+    jmp     %%asked
+%%spins:
+    xor     r13d, r13d
+%%asked:
+%endmacro
+
+;
+; ap_sleeps: halt, and say that interrupts are off, which the host ends for
+; a kick alone. A kick that came since this AP last halted ends the halt at
+; once, so none is missed between a look at the mailbox and the halt. Uses
+; RAX, RCX and R10 to R12.
+;
+%macro ap_sleeps 0
+    xor     eax, eax
+    mov     ecx, 0x1c00
+    xor     r10d, r10d
+    mov     r11d, TDVMCALL_HLT
+    mov     r12d, 1
+    tdcall
+%endmacro
+
+;
+; ap_kicks_all: kick every APIC ID below the TD's MAX_VCPUS, which R8 holds
+; in its upper half, where R14 is 1: the host wakes a halted vCPU for a
+; kick. Uses RAX, RCX, R10 to R12 and R15.
+;
+%macro ap_kicks_all 0
+    test    r14d, r14d
+    jz      %%kicked
+    xor     r15d, r15d
+%%next:
+    mov     rax, r8
+    shr     rax, 32
+    cmp     r15d, eax
+    jae     %%kicked
+    xor     eax, eax
+    mov     ecx, 0x1c00
+    mov     r10d, KVM_HC_KICK_CPU
+    xor     r11d, r11d
+    mov     r12d, r15d
+    tdcall
+    inc     r15d
+    jmp     %%next
+%%kicked:
+%endmacro
+
+;
+; ap_waits: wait to look again at what this AP waits for: asleep where R13
+; is 1, and for a pause where it is not. Uses RAX, RCX and R10 to R12.
+;
+%macro ap_waits 0
+    test    r13d, r13d
+    jz      %%pauses
+    ap_sleeps
+    jmp     %%waited
+%%pauses:
+    pause
+%%waited:
+%endmacro
--- a/OvmfPkg/IntelTdx/Sec/X64/IntelTdxAPs.nasm
+++ b/OvmfPkg/IntelTdx/Sec/X64/IntelTdxAPs.nasm
@@ -17,6 +17,22 @@
     ; R9  [31:0]  VCPU_INDEX
     ;
 ParkAp:
+    ;
+    ; R14 says from here on whether this AP's host wakes a halted vCPU for a
+    ; kick, and R13 whether this AP sleeps as it waits. The BSP kicks the
+    ; APIC IDs below MAX_VCPUS, so an AP whose ID is none of them spins.
+    ;
+    ap_may_sleep
+    mov       ebx, r13d
+    ap_asks_cpuid 0xb
+    mov       r14d, ebx
+    mov       r13d, 0
+    jnz       do_wait_loop
+    mov       rax, r8
+    shr       rax, 32
+    cmp       r15d, eax
+    jae       do_wait_loop
+    mov       r13d, r14d
 
 do_wait_loop:
     ;
@@ -26,16 +42,26 @@
     lock xadd dword [rsp + CpuArrivalOffset], eax
     inc       eax
 
+    ;
+    ; The AP whose arrival makes the count whole wakes those that sleep on
+    ; it: the BSP kicked when it counted itself, which may have been before.
+    ;
+    cmp       eax, r8d
+    jne       .check_arrival_cnt
+    ap_kicks_all
+    jmp       .check_command
+
 .check_arrival_cnt:
     cmp       eax, r8d
     je        .check_command
+    ap_waits
     mov       eax, dword[rsp + CpuArrivalOffset]
     jmp       .check_arrival_cnt
 
 .check_command:
     mov     eax, dword[rsp + CommandOffset]
     cmp     eax, MpProtectedModeWakeupCommandNoop
-    je      .check_command
+    je      .wait_command
 
     cmp     eax, MpProtectedModeWakeupCommandWakeup
     je      .do_wakeup
@@ -44,6 +70,9 @@
     je      .do_accept_pages
 
     ; Don't support this command, so ignore
+
+.wait_command:
+    ap_waits
     jmp     .check_command
 
 .do_accept_pages:
@@ -101,6 +130,7 @@
 .check_exiting_cnt:
     cmp     eax, 0
     je      do_wait_loop
+    ap_waits
     mov     eax, dword[rsp + CpusExitingOffset]
     jmp     .check_exiting_cnt
 
--- a/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/TdxMailbox.c
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/TdxMailbox.c
@@ -18,6 +18,38 @@
 #include <Library/TdxMailboxLib.h>
 
 /**
+  Kick the vCPUs whose APIC ID is below Limit, where the TD's host wakes a
+  halted vCPU for a kick. Under a host that does not, it does nothing.
+
+  @param[in] Limit            The first APIC ID that is not kicked
+**/
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+TdxMailboxKick (
+  IN UINT32  Limit
+  );
+
+/**
+  This function will be called by BSP to wake the APs that sleep at a mailbox,
+  after it has changed what they wait for (see TdxCommondefs.inc). An AP
+  whose APIC ID this does not reach does not sleep where the BSP wakes it.
+**/
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+MpKickAps (
+  VOID
+  )
+{
+  UINT64          Status;
+  TD_RETURN_DATA  TdReturnData;
+
+  Status = TdCall (TDCALL_TDINFO, 0, 0, 0, &TdReturnData);
+  if (Status == TDX_EXIT_REASON_SUCCESS) {
+    TdxMailboxKick (TdReturnData.TdInfo.MaxVcpus);
+  }
+}
+
+/**
   This function will be called by BSP to get the CPU number.
 
   @retval   CPU number
@@ -87,6 +119,7 @@
   AsmCpuid (0x01, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
   MailBox->Command = Command;
   AsmCpuid (0x01, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
+  MpKickAps ();
   return;
 }
 
@@ -113,6 +146,7 @@
   DEBUG ((DEBUG_VERBOSE, "Releasing APs\n"));
   MailBox->NumCpusExiting = NumOfCpus;
   InterlockedIncrement ((UINT32 *)&MailBox->NumCpusArriving);
+  MpKickAps ();
 }
 
 /**
@@ -136,4 +170,5 @@
   MailBox->Command         = MpProtectedModeWakeupCommandNoop;
   MailBox->NumCpusArriving = 0;
   InterlockedDecrement ((UINT32 *)&MailBox->NumCpusExiting);
+  MpKickAps ();
 }
--- a/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/TdxMailboxLib.inf
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/TdxMailboxLib.inf
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 
 [Sources.X64]
   TdxMailbox.c
+  X64/TdxMailboxKick.nasm
 
 [Packages]
   MdePkg/MdePkg.dec
--- a/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/TdxMailboxNull.c
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/TdxMailboxNull.c
@@ -83,3 +83,16 @@
 {
   ASSERT (FALSE);
 }
+
+/**
+  This function will be called by BSP to wake the APs that sleep at a mailbox,
+  after it has changed what they wait for.
+**/
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+MpKickAps (
+  VOID
+  )
+{
+  ASSERT (FALSE);
+}
--- /dev/null
+++ b/OvmfPkg/Library/TdxMailboxLib/X64/TdxMailboxKick.nasm
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+;------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+; @file
+; The BSP's kick of the APs that sleep at the mailbox
+;
+; SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause-Patent
+;
+;------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+%include "TdxCommondefs.inc"
+
+DEFAULT REL
+SECTION .text
+
+BITS 64
+
+%macro  tdcall  0
+  db  0x66, 0x0f, 0x01, 0xcc
+%endmacro
+
+;------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+;  VOID
+;  EFIAPI
+;  TdxMailboxKick (
+;    IN UINT32  Limit   // Rcx
+;    );
+;
+;  Kicks every APIC ID below Limit, the caller's own among them, which costs
+;  the caller one halt that returns at once. The host is shown R10 to R12
+;  of a kick, and R10 to R15 of the two CPUID leaves that say whether it
+;  takes one.
+;------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+global ASM_PFX(TdxMailboxKick)
+ASM_PFX(TdxMailboxKick):
+    push    rbx
+    push    r12
+    push    r13
+    push    r14
+    push    r15
+
+    mov     ebx, ecx
+    ap_may_sleep
+    test    r13d, r13d
+    jz      .done
+
+    xor     r13d, r13d
+.next:
+    cmp     r13d, ebx
+    jae     .done
+    xor     eax, eax
+    mov     ecx, 0x1c00
+    mov     r10d, KVM_HC_KICK_CPU
+    xor     r11d, r11d
+    mov     r12d, r13d
+    tdcall
+    inc     r13d
+    jmp     .next
+
+.done:
+    pop     r15
+    pop     r14
+    pop     r13
+    pop     r12
+    pop     rbx
+    ret
--- a/OvmfPkg/ResetVector/Ia32/IntelTdx.nasm.inc
+++ b/OvmfPkg/ResetVector/Ia32/IntelTdx.nasm.inc
@@ -103,8 +103,9 @@
     ;
 TdxApWait:
     cmp     byte[TDX_WORK_AREA_PGTBL_READY], 0
-    je      TdxApWait
-    jmp     ExitInitTdxWorkarea
+    jne     ExitInitTdxWorkarea
+    pause
+    jmp     TdxApWait
 
 TdxBspEntry:
     ;
--- a/OvmfPkg/TdxDxe/TdxAcpiTable.c
+++ b/OvmfPkg/TdxDxe/TdxAcpiTable.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include <Protocol/AcpiTable.h>
 #include <Library/UefiBootServicesTableLib.h>
 #include <Library/UefiLib.h>
+#include <Guid/EventGroup.h>
 #include <Library/TdxMailboxLib.h>
 #include <Protocol/Cpu.h>
 #include <Uefi.h>
@@ -139,10 +140,16 @@
   RelocatedMailBox->Command      = MpProtectedModeWakeupCommandNoop;
   RelocatedMailBox->ApicId       = MP_CPU_PROTECTED_MODE_MAILBOX_APICID_INVALID;
   RelocatedMailBox->WakeUpVector = 0;
+  //
+  // Until boot services end, an AP sleeps at this mailbox, and
+  // HandOverMailBox wakes it. The mailbox's area for the OS is zero, as the
+  // whole page is: an OS that kicks says so there.
+  //
+  RelocatedMailBox->FirmwareRuns = 1;
 
   //
   // Wakup APs and have been move to the finalized run loop
-  // They will spin until guest OS wakes them
+  // They will wait there until guest OS wakes them
   //
   MpSerializeStart ();
 
@@ -166,6 +173,31 @@
 }
 
 /**
+  Hand the relocated mailbox over to the OS as boot services end. From here
+  on an OS sets its commands, and an AP sleeps at it only under an OS that
+  has said it kicks: the APs are woken to find that out.
+
+  @param[in]  Event     Event whose notification function is being invoked
+  @param[in]  Context   The relocated mailbox
+**/
+STATIC
+VOID
+EFIAPI
+HandOverMailBox (
+  IN EFI_EVENT  Event,
+  IN VOID       *Context
+  )
+{
+  //
+  // The word first, then the kicks: an AP that read the word as set and
+  // has not halted yet is kicked after, and its halt returns at once.
+  //
+  ((volatile MP_WAKEUP_MAILBOX *)Context)->FirmwareRuns = 0;
+  MemoryFence ();
+  MpKickAps ();
+}
+
+/**
   Alter the MADT when ACPI Table from QEMU is available.
 
   @param[in]  Event     Event whose notification function is being invoked
@@ -192,6 +224,7 @@
   EFI_PHYSICAL_ADDRESS                                 RelocateResetVector;
   EFI_ACPI_6_6_MULTIPROCESSOR_WAKEUP_STRUCTURE         *MadtMpWk;
   EFI_ACPI_1_0_MULTIPLE_APIC_DESCRIPTION_TABLE_HEADER  *MadtHeader;
+  EFI_EVENT                                            HandOverEvent;
 
   Index        = 0;
   NewMadtTable = NULL;
@@ -208,6 +241,23 @@
     ASSERT (FALSE);
     DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "Failed to relocate Td mailbox\n"));
     return;
+  }
+
+  //
+  // The APs sleep at the relocated mailbox until boot services end. With
+  // nobody to wake them then, they are woken now and spin.
+  //
+  Status = gBS->CreateEventEx (
+                  EVT_NOTIFY_SIGNAL,
+                  TPL_CALLBACK,
+                  HandOverMailBox,
+                  (VOID *)(UINTN)RelocateMailboxAddress,
+                  &gEfiEventExitBootServicesGuid,
+                  &HandOverEvent
+                  );
+  if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "No event for the end of boot services: the APs spin. %r\n", Status));
+    HandOverMailBox (NULL, (VOID *)(UINTN)RelocateMailboxAddress);
   }
 
   do {
--- a/OvmfPkg/TdxDxe/TdxDxe.inf
+++ b/OvmfPkg/TdxDxe/TdxDxe.inf
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@
 
 [Guids]
   gUefiOvmfPkgPlatformInfoGuid                      ## CONSUMES
+  gEfiEventExitBootServicesGuid                     ## CONSUMES ## Event
 
 [Protocols]
   gQemuAcpiTableNotifyProtocolGuid                 ## CONSUMES
--- a/OvmfPkg/TdxDxe/X64/ApRunLoop.nasm
+++ b/OvmfPkg/TdxDxe/X64/ApRunLoop.nasm
@@ -92,22 +92,56 @@
     jnz         Panic
     mov         r8, r15
     mov         qword[rel mailbox_address], rbx
+    ; From here on RSI says whether the kick the BSP sends as boot services
+    ; end reaches this AP, which it does where the AP's APIC ID is below the
+    ; TD's MAX_VCPUS, and R13 whether this AP's host can wake it from a halt.
+    mov         rdi, r8
+    mov         rax, TDCALL_TDINFO
+    tdcall
+    shr         r8, 32
+    xor         esi, esi
+    cmp         edi, r8d
+    setb        sil
+    mov         r8, rdi
+    ap_may_sleep
+    and         esi, r13d
 
 MailBoxLoop:
-    ; Spin until command set
+    ; Wait until command set
     cmp        dword [rbx + CommandOffset], MULTIPROCESSOR_WAKEUP_MAILBOX_COMMAND_NOOP
-    je         MailBoxLoop
+    je         MailBoxWait
     ; Determine if this is a broadcast or directly for my apic-id, if not, ignore
     cmp        dword [rbx + ApicidOffset], MailboxApicidBroadcast
     je         MailBoxProcessCommand
     cmp        dword [rbx + ApicidOffset], r8d
-    jne        MailBoxLoop
+    jne        MailBoxWait
 MailBoxProcessCommand:
     cmp        dword [rbx + CommandOffset], MULTIPROCESSOR_WAKEUP_MAILBOX_COMMAND_WAKEUP
     je         MailBoxWakeUp
     cmp        dword [rbx + CommandOffset], MULTIPROCESSOR_WAKEUP_MAILBOX_COMMAND_TEST
     je         MailBoxTest
     ; Don't support this command, so ignore
+MailBoxWait:
+    ; Asleep only where somebody will wake this AP. While the firmware runs
+    ; that is the BSP, as it hands the machine over, where its kick reaches
+    ; this AP. After that it is an OS that has said it kicks an AP after it
+    ; sets a command, where the host can wake this AP. Otherwise spin.
+    cmp        qword [rbx + FirmwareRunsOffset], 0
+    je         MailBoxUnderOs
+    test       esi, esi
+    jnz        MailBoxSleep
+    jmp        MailBoxSpin
+MailBoxUnderOs:
+    test       r13d, r13d
+    jz         MailBoxSpin
+    mov        rax, MP_WAKEUP_OS_KICKS
+    cmp        qword [rbx + OSArgsOffset], rax
+    jne        MailBoxSpin
+MailBoxSleep:
+    ap_sleeps
+    jmp        MailBoxLoop
+MailBoxSpin:
+    pause
     jmp        MailBoxLoop
 MailBoxWakeUp:
     mov        rax, [rbx + WakeupVectorOffset]
